  abstract = {A Bayesian mixture model for high dimensional non-stationary time series is presented that accommodates non-standard measurements that are mixtures of continuous and discrete distributions. A coherent probabilistic approach allows the short-term, long-term and contemporaneous dependencies, and their associated uncertainties, to be modelled simultaneously. Dimension reduction is achieved by encoding these dependencies within a mixture-of-experts framework in a novel manner. Recent advances in data augmentation methods (Polson et al., 2013) permit an MCMC sampler for this model that is computationally efficient, does not need tuning, and can run in a distributed computing context. The synergism of dimension reduction and computational efficiency is key as we apply the method to daily rainfall across the entire Australian continent, a large data set comprising around 300 million time series measurements distributed across 17,321 sites. The method allows for a large variety of inferential procedures in the form of point estimates and probabilistic intervals, of which the paper includes short-term daily component classification (e.g., no/low/high rainfall events), monthly intensity levels and long-term rainfall trends across the entire continent. Evidence of good model performance is provided. The sampling scheme is implemented as an R package and is available upon request.},
