  abstract = {The well known label switching problem complicates Bayesian inference for mixture models. A mixture model with k components has k! equivalent modes, rendering posterior means that average over the entire posterior distribution meaningless. A traditional solution involves setting constraints on the parameter space to identify a single mode of interest. Alternatively, constraints can be set on the space of missing indicators for membership in the components of the mixture distribution. Such constraints imply an asymmetric prior distribution for the parameter space that dampens the nuisance modes and leaves the mode of interest almost unchanged. The method involves the dynamic allocation of as few as k - 1 cases in the MCMC posterior simulation to ``known'' components. Evaluated in the context of a 3-component mixture of exponentials, the technique performs as well as traditional methods using optimal constraints. The technique is also illustrated in an analysis of the time spent studying on a web-based study tool by students preparing to take the GRE.},
