  abstract = {The Belousov-Zhabotinskii reaction is a chemical reaction which exhibits both temporal and spatial oscillations. The reaction is essentially the oxidation of malonic acid, for example, by bromate in the presence of a cerium catalyst. The oscillations both temporal and spatial, travelling concentration waves in fact, are accompanied by dramatic colour changes. Its importance is as a simple (compared with enzymatic oscillating systems) practical example which may mirror more complicated biological oscillators. A kinetic model, based on the detailed chemical kinetics, has been suggested by Field and Noyes, 1974a, Field and Noyes, 1974b to explain the oscillations. The reaction and model are described in detail here. For spatial oscillations the model results in a non-linear pair of partial differential equations which this paper is mainly about. It is shown that wave solutions, which correspond to moving bands of concentration in the reaction, of the system of equations exist and various relevant properties of them obtained. With parameter values obtained from experiment, numerical results are given for the travelling wave solutions. The wave speed as a function of the various parameters for a wide range of experimentally relevant values is also given. The results are compared with experiment and reasonable agreement is found.},
